Note: This document was split out of the [Local Peer-to-Peer API](https://wicg.github.io/local-peer-to-peer/) specification. It is an early, unofficial draft that sets a direction. Open questions are marked as issues.

Issue(34): Local HTTPS is a work in progress.

Introduction {#intro}
=====================

*This section is non-normative.*

Browsers can't reach a NAS, printer or TV on the local network over HTTPS without a public name, a certificate authority and an internet connection. Local HTTPS uses ordinary X.509 certificate path validation [[RFC5280]] instead, with two differences from the Web PKI:

1. **Trust anchors are added by the user** through a Local Peer-to-Peer [=trust ceremony=], instead of a root program. Each anchor is limited to a namespace under `.local`.
2. **Requests from a page are scoped** to the anchors for devices that the page was granted.

The origin model, CORS, the `Origin` header and [[LOCAL-NETWORK-ACCESS|Local Network Access]] are unchanged.

Trust anchors {#trust-anchors}
==============================

Local HTTPS uses the Local Peer-to-Peer [=local trust store=], one per user profile, separate from the Web PKI root store. Its [=trust anchors=] are added through a [=trust ceremony=]. For Local HTTPS, each [=trust anchor=] has a <dfn>namespace</dfn>, derived from its [=trust anchor/scope=] and kept as name constraints in the trust store entry, as in [[RFC5914]]:

- for [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], the name that peer advertises and the names under it;
- for [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=], the namespace the user accepted in the [=trust ceremony=].

<div class="example">
- A device that advertises its services under `nas` is an anchor for `nas.local` and `*.nas.local`.
- A referee, such as a home hub that vouches for other devices, is an anchor for `*.home.local`.

</div>

The namespace comes from the name the device advertises, and is trusted only once the ceremony has authenticated the device. Anchors with the same key merge as [=add a trust anchor=] does: a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] anchor replaces the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] anchors with the same key. Anchors with different keys MUST NOT both be able to certify a host: the most specific [=namespace=] that contains a host decides which anchor may certify it, as with DNS delegation. Local HTTPS defines a [=trust anchor checks|trust anchor check=] that returns false for an anchor that would change which key certifies a host already in use, such as that of a [=known peer=] or a host with site data, so the [=trust ceremony=] fails. So at most one key can certify a given host.

Issue: Define how the ceremony conveys and confirms the namespace, for devices and for referees.

Issue: Rotating an anchor key needs a proof of continuity, which is out of scope.

Local origins {#local-origins}
==============================

A <dfn>local host</dfn> is a [=/host=] that is a domain under `local.`, which is resolved by Multicast DNS and never by global DNS [[RFC6762]].

A URL targets a <dfn>local origin</dfn> if its host is a [=local host=] within the [=namespace=] of one of the request's [=applicable anchors=]. If its scheme is `http`, the user agent upgrades it to `https`, as for a known HSTS host. For any other URL, behaviour is unchanged.

Issue: Should other special-use local domains, such as `home.arpa` [[RFC8375]], be included?

Validation {#validation}
------------------------

The user agent finds the address of a [=local origin=] through discovery, which is untrusted, as DNS is. It sends the host in SNI and validates the certificate path [[RFC5280]] with:

- the anchor with the most specific [=namespace=] that contains the host as the trust anchor;
- that [=namespace=] and, for a request from a page, its [=grant constraint=] as initial name constraints;
- for a [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] anchor, the peer it is for: a path that certifies another peer under the same key fails;
- the host as the reference identifier, matched against the subjectAltName [[RFC9525]].

Certificate errors for a [=local origin=] MUST NOT be overridable. Leaves and intermediates can be short-lived and rotate freely.

Issue: Should the user agent also authenticate as a client? The requesting origin would still only be asserted.

Upgrade signal {#upgrade-signal}
--------------------------------

A device might signal that it supports Local HTTPS, and which anchor it chains to, so that the user agent can try the upgrade or offer the matching trust ceremony. Such a signal would only be a hint and MUST NOT add an anchor or affect validation.

Issue: Define whether and how a device signals support. Candidates are tied to HTTP or TLS, for example a response header on `http:` in the spirit of HSTS and Alt-Svc, or the certificate chain presented when an `https:` connection is attempted. The IETF [SETTLE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/settle/about/) working group may define one.

Scopes {#scopes}
================

The <dfn>applicable anchors</dfn> for a request are:

- for a navigation the user initiated, such as from the address bar or a bookmark, every [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] anchor in the [=local trust store=], and every [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] anchor for the hosts of [=known peers=] that chain to it. For another host in the [=namespace=] of a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] anchor, whose device is [=unconfirmed=], the user agent first asks the user to confirm the device, naming the referee, and adds it as a [=known peer=] if they do;
- for a request from an [=/origin=], only the anchors that the peers it was granted through [=acquire a local peer grant=] chain to: the anchors with their recorded [=known peer/anchor key=]. A [=peer grants|peer grant=] made by accepting an incoming session, which has a requesting origin, does not count.

The <dfn>grant constraint</dfn> of a request from an [=/origin=] is derived from the same peers: for each, the name it advertises and the names under it, such as `nas.local` and `*.nas.local`. It acts as an extra name constraint, so a page granted one device can't reach another device under the same referee.

The user agent MUST NOT resolve, connect to or probe a [=local host=] for a page outside its [=applicable anchors=].

Issue: Which scope applies to navigations that a page starts?

Issue: Is a peer grant keyed by origin or by top-level site?

Origins, Fetch and Local Network Access {#origins}
==================================================

A [=local origin=] has the usual tuple, for example (`https`, `nas.local`, 443), and is a secure context. Because at most one key can certify a host, the tuple is unambiguous within a profile. A different `nas` device fails validation, and an unpaired `http://nas.local` is a different origin.

When a [=trust anchor=] is removed, the user agent MUST clear site data for all hosts in its [=namespace=].

Requests to [=local origins=] are ordinary requests [[FETCH]]: servers apply CORS based on the `Origin` header, and WebTransport servers check `Origin` [[WEBTRANSPORT]]. The [=peer grants=] satisfy the [[LOCAL-NETWORK-ACCESS|Local Network Access]] permission only for the hosts within the [=grant constraint=]. CSP is not extended: `connect-src https://*.local` works.

Note: Unlike ["Expanding what HTTPS means"](https://lowentropy.net/posts/local-https/), this doesn't add the server's key to the origin tuple; allowing at most one key per host already makes names unambiguous.

User interface {#ui}
====================

For a top-level [=local origin=], the user agent SHOULD show a "paired device" indicator with the host, and the referee if any (for example "via Home"), outside the content area.

The user agent MUST NOT show a name supplied by the device unless it obtained the name after the [=trust ceremony=] authenticated the device.

Examples {#examples}
====================

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class="example" id="example-services">
A device with several services: a daemon on the NAS holds the anchor key and acts as a local intermediate, issuing leaves to `print.nas.local` and `media.nas.local`. A page granted the NAS can use both.
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-referee">
A referee, as in REQ-ARCH-02 of the [SETTLE requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-sweet-settle-requirements/): the user pairs once with a home hub for `*.home.local`. The hub issues a device intermediate for `*.nas.home.local`, which issues service leaves. New devices work without pairing each one; the user confirms each on first use.
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-matter">
A Matter fabric: a browser that joins a fabric as an ordinary node, as any Matter node does, trusts that fabric's root. That root could act as an anchor for a namespace for the fabric, such as `*.matter.local`.
</div>

Security and privacy considerations {#security-and-privacy-considerations}
==========================================================================

- **Only the key is verified.** A valid path proves that the server holds a certificate chaining to an anchor the user accepted for that namespace, and nothing more.
- **URLs are per user.** `https://nas.local` may mean a different device, or nothing, for another user.
- **No probing.** Pages can't reach or detect devices outside their [=applicable anchors=].
- **Key theft.** A stolen anchor key compromises its namespace until the user removes the anchor. A compromised referee controls every name under it, hence explicit consent, confirmation of each device on first use, and a visible indicator. A referee never widens a page's grants.
- **Linkability.** A paired device can recognize the same browser across sites. That is inherent to pairing.
